# REQUIRES: clang, llvm-as, llvm-dis, x86-target
#
# RELEASE CARRIER SURVIVAL AT THE BITCODE LEVEL.
#
# integration/release-carrier-inline-survival.test shows the MLIR inliner honours
# passthrough noinline. This test asks the same question one level down, where it
# actually matters: does the carrier survive into the FROZEN BITCODE that the
# binder and the carrier auditor read?
#
# Profile section 4.4 requires "a direct call to a manifest-named outlined
# release wrapper" with final call-occurrence cardinality matching the manifest.
# Both halves of that are bitcode-level facts: the call must be there, and the
# COUNT must be right.
#
# These checks make no ModelStatus claim.

# --- pinned wrapper survives O2, but default tail calls are out of profile ---
# RUN: %clang -std=c11 -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -fno-builtin --target=x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu -O2 -c -emit-llvm %harness/c/release_carrier.c -o %t.rc.bc
# RUN: %llvm-dis %t.rc.bc -o %t.rc.ll
# RUN: %FileCheck %s --check-prefix=CARRIER --input-file=%t.rc.ll

# The wrapper is still a defined function and still carries noinline.
# CARRIER-DAG: define{{.*}}@sps_release_p_v1
# CARRIER-DAG: noinline
# Clang's default sibling-call optimization marks both occurrences `tail`.
# V1 rejects tail/musttail/notail/invoke/callbr carriers, so mere survival is
# not enough to establish an admissible carrier.
# CARRIER: tail call i32 @sps_release_p_v1

# --- a non-tail call-shape arm has cardinality exactly two ------------------
# This isolates call kind and occurrence count. It is not a fully admissible
# carrier because C still cannot express all of the required optimizer pins.
# RUN: %clang -std=c11 -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -fno-builtin -fno-optimize-sibling-calls --target=x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu -O2 -c -emit-llvm %harness/c/release_carrier.c -o %t.rc.nontail.bc
# RUN: %llvm-dis %t.rc.nontail.bc -o %t.rc.nontail.ll
# RUN: %FileCheck %s --check-prefix=NONTAIL --implicit-check-not='tail call i32 @sps_release_p_v1' --input-file=%t.rc.nontail.ll
# NONTAIL: {{^  %[^ ]+ = call i32 @sps_release_p_v1}}
# NONTAIL: {{^  %[^ ]+ = call i32 @sps_release_p_v1}}
# NONTAIL-NOT: call i32 @sps_release_p_v1

# The manifest for this fixture declares multiplicity 2. If the optimiser had
# merged, cloned, or inlined either site the count would move, and the carrier
# audit would have nothing stable to bind. Asserted as a count rather than a
# presence check, because presence is the easy half.
# RUN: grep -c "call i32 @sps_release_p_v1" %t.rc.nontail.ll | %FileCheck %s --check-prefix=CALLCOUNT
# CALLCOUNT: 2

# --- and it survives canonicalisation ---------------------------------------
# The binder reads the reparsed canonical module, not the emitted one, so the
# carrier has to be present after the round trip too.
# RUN: %llvm-as %t.rc.nontail.ll -o %t.rc.canon.bc
# RUN: %llvm-dis %t.rc.canon.bc -o %t.rc.canon.ll
# RUN: not grep -q "tail call i32 @sps_release_p_v1" %t.rc.canon.ll
# RUN: grep -c "call i32 @sps_release_p_v1" %t.rc.canon.ll | %FileCheck %s --check-prefix=CANON-CALLCOUNT
# CANON-CALLCOUNT: 2

# --- the negative arm: without the pin, the carrier is gone -----------------
# Same source, same flags, with the attribute removed by preprocessor
# substitution. This is the C-level counterpart of the MLIR differential.
# RUN: sed 's/__attribute__((noinline))//' %harness/c/release_carrier.c > %t.unpinned.c
# RUN: %clang -std=c11 -fno-builtin --target=x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu -O2 -c -emit-llvm %t.unpinned.c -o %t.unpinned.bc
# RUN: %llvm-dis %t.unpinned.bc -o %t.unpinned.ll
# RUN: %FileCheck %s --check-prefix=UNPINNED --implicit-check-not='call i32 @sps_release_p_v1' --input-file=%t.unpinned.ll

# No call remains; the mask arithmetic has been spliced into the caller.
# UNPINNED: and i32

# INTERPRETATION.
#
# The unpinned arm is not a leak. It computes and publishes the same values. What
# it has lost is bindability: no direct call to the manifest-named wrapper means
# no stable SiteId, no ABI-role mapping, and no occurrence cardinality to match.
# The correct disposition is a refusal, not a counterexample.
#
# A LIMITATION THIS TEST MAKES VISIBLE. Only `noinline` is pinned here, because
# only `noinline` is expressible from C -- Clang warns and ignores GCC's
# `noclone`, and there is no portable C spelling for LLVM's `noduplicate`. So a
# release wrapper declared purely in C is pinned against inlining and against
# nothing else; `nomerge`, `noduplicate` and the outliner pin have to be applied
# at IR level. A declassifier API that ships as a C header is therefore an
# incomplete carrier by construction, and that is worth knowing before designing
# one.
